import{_ as t,o,c as d,ao as a}from"./chunks/framework.VZIxSky_.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"libfftl C API","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/libfftl.md","filePath":"reference/libfftl.md","lastUpdated":null}'),l={name:"reference/libfftl.md"};function r(n,e,i,s,c,f){return o(),d("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="libfftl-C-API" tabindex="-1">libfftl C API <a class="header-anchor" href="#libfftl-C-API" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;libfftl C API {#libfftl-C-API}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>libfftl&#39;s interface is FFTW3&#39;s, as the <a href="https://www.fftw.org/fftw3_doc/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">FFTW manual</a> describes it; the manual is the reference for every function, argument and convention. libfftl&#39;s own header files are <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR6/generated/libfftl_fftw3_header#C-header-(fftw3.h)"><code>fftw3.h</code></a>, with the list of differences at its top, and <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR6/generated/libfftl_header#C-header-(fftl.h)"><code>fftl.h</code></a> for the two functions FFTW has no counterpart for. The Fortran include files <code>fftw3.f</code> and <code>fftw3.f03</code> are in the same directory (<code>libfftl/include</code>). This page lists what is supported, what differs and what is missing.</p><h2 id="Supported" tabindex="-1">Supported <a class="header-anchor" href="#Supported" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Supported {#Supported}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every function below exists in double (<code>fftw_</code>) and single precision (<code>fftwf_</code>), from one library:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">group</th><th style="text-align:left;">functions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">basic planners</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>plan_dft{,_1d,_2d,_3d}</code>, <code>plan_dft_r2c{,_1d,_2d,_3d}</code>, <code>plan_dft_c2r{,_1d,_2d,_3d}</code>, <code>plan_r2r{,_1d,_2d,_3d}</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">advanced planners</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>plan_many_dft</code>, <code>plan_many_dft_r2c</code>, <code>plan_many_dft_c2r</code>, <code>plan_many_r2r</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">guru planners</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>plan_guru{,64}_dft</code>, <code>_dft_r2c</code>, <code>_dft_c2r</code>, <code>_r2r</code>, and the split-array <code>plan_guru{,64}_split_dft</code>, <code>_split_dft_r2c</code>, <code>_split_dft_c2r</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">execution</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>execute</code>, <code>execute_dft</code>, <code>execute_dft_r2c</code>, <code>execute_dft_c2r</code>, <code>execute_r2r</code>, <code>execute_split_dft</code>, <code>execute_split_dft_r2c</code>, <code>execute_split_dft_c2r</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">plans</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>destroy_plan</code>, <code>copy_plan</code>, <code>cleanup</code>, <code>set_timelimit</code>, <code>cost</code>, <code>flops</code>, <code>print_plan</code>, <code>fprint_plan</code>, <code>sprint_plan</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">memory</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>malloc</code>, <code>free</code>, <code>alloc_real</code>, <code>alloc_complex</code>, <code>alignment_of</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">wisdom</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>export_wisdom{,_to_filename,_to_file,_to_string}</code>, <code>import_wisdom{,_from_filename,_from_file,_from_string}</code>, <code>import_system_wisdom</code>, <code>forget_wisdom</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">threads</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>init_threads</code>, <code>plan_with_nthreads</code>, <code>planner_nthreads</code>, <code>cleanup_threads</code>, <code>make_planner_thread_safe</code>, <code>threads_set_callback</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>All eleven real-to-real kinds (<code>FFTW_R2HC</code>, <code>FFTW_HC2R</code>, <code>FFTW_DHT</code>, <code>FFTW_REDFT00</code> … <code>FFTW_REDFT11</code>, <code>FFTW_RODFT00</code> … <code>FFTW_RODFT11</code>), any rank, any lengths (lengths with large prime factors use Bluestein&#39;s algorithm), and every layout the manual describes: strides, embedded arrays, vector loops, different input and output layouts, in place and out of place, interleaved and split complex arrays.</p><p>The legacy Fortran interface has the routines the manual gives it: every function above without <code>FILE</code>, string or memory arguments, as subroutines <code>dfftw_…</code> and <code>sfftw_…</code> with the return value as first argument, each with one and with two trailing underscores. The Fortran 2003 interface (<code>fftw3.f03</code>) declares the C functions for <code>iso_c_binding</code>; as the manual describes, it leaves out <code>fftw_execute</code>.</p><p>libfftl&#39;s own functions (<code>fftl.h</code>):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">function</th><th style="text-align:left;">purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>fftl_last_error(buf, len)</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the message of the last failed call on this thread: every <code>NULL</code> plan, rejected executions and failed allocations leave one</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>fftl_version(buf, len)</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the library version as <code>&quot;major.minor.patch&quot;</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="What-differs-from-FFTW" tabindex="-1">What differs from FFTW <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-differs-from-FFTW" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What differs from FFTW {#What-differs-from-FFTW}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The list at the top of <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR6/generated/libfftl_fftw3_header#C-header-(fftw3.h)"><code>fftw3.h</code></a> is the complete one. In short:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Layouts outside one contiguous array are copied.</strong> Plans whose input and output form one dense array (in any order of dimensions; for r2c/c2r FFTW&#39;s padded in-place layout, with the halved dimension the fastest of the transform dimensions) run on the arrays. All other layouts, and all split arrays, are gathered into a buffer of the plan and scattered back, one extra pass over the input and one over the output.</p></li><li><p><strong>Planner flags change nothing</strong>, except <code>FFTW_WISDOM_ONLY</code>, which gives <code>NULL</code> because there is never wisdom. Planning never touches the arrays. Out-of-place c2r keeps its input.</p></li><li><p><strong>Wisdom.</strong> Export writes a placeholder; import accepts any input and returns 1 (0 for a file that cannot be opened); <code>fftw_import_system_wisdom</code> returns 0.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>fftw_cost</code> and <code>fftw_flops</code></strong> return an estimate (5 N log₂ N for a complex DFT of N points, half of it for real transforms), not a count of FFTL&#39;s operations.</p></li><li><p><strong>Alignment does not matter</strong>: <code>fftw_alignment_of</code> returns 0 for every pointer, and the new-array execute functions accept arrays of any alignment. <code>fftw_malloc</code> aligns to 64 bytes.</p></li><li><p><strong>Threads.</strong> The worker pool is fixed when the library is built (<code>FFTL_THREADS</code>, see <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR6/howto/libfftl#C-library-(libfftl)">C library (libfftl)</a>); <code>fftw_plan_with_nthreads</code> chooses how many of its threads later plans use. <code>fftw_threads_set_callback</code> is accepted and ignored.</p></li><li><p><strong>Leniency.</strong> Some calls that FFTW rejects or leaves undefined have a defined result: in-place split r2c/c2r of rank above 1 is planned, plans survive <code>fftw_cleanup</code>, and execute functions check the plan, its transform type and the in-place-ness of the arrays and set <code>fftl_last_error</code> instead of running.</p></li><li><p><strong>Split plans</strong> execute through <code>fftw_execute</code> or the split execute functions only, and interleaved plans through the interleaved ones.</p></li></ul><h2 id="What-is-missing" tabindex="-1">What is missing <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-is-missing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is missing {#What-is-missing}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">item</th><th style="text-align:left;">why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">the variables <code>fftw_version</code>, <code>fftw_cc</code>, <code>fftw_codelet_optim</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>juliac</code> exports functions only; a program that reads them does not link against libfftl</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>fftw_estimate_cost</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">not described in the manual</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">long double (<code>fftwl_</code>) and quad precision (<code>fftwq_</code>)</td><td style="text-align:left;">FFTL&#39;s Julia API supports any <code>AbstractFloat</code>, but the library is built for <code>float</code> and <code>double</code> only</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">MPI (<code>fftw_mpi_</code>)</td><td style="text-align:left;">no distributed-memory transforms</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">FFTW&#39;s wisdom format</td><td style="text-align:left;">the manual does not specify it; libfftl&#39;s export cannot be read by FFTW</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#Limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits {#Limits}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;"></th><th style="text-align:left;">limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">sizes</td><td style="text-align:left;">any rank, any lengths ≥ 1 for transform dimensions, ≥ 0 for vector dimensions (0: a plan that does nothing)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">strides</td><td style="text-align:left;">any, including negative ones</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">normalisation</td><td style="text-align:left;">none; the backward transform multiplies by the number of transformed elements</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">memory</td><td style="text-align:left;">bounded by the available memory and by 64-bit element offsets</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">concurrency</td><td style="text-align:left;">plans are created, executed and destroyed from any thread; one plan may run on several threads at once through the new-array execute functions; destroying a plan during its execution is not allowed</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">number types</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>float</code> and <code>double</code> only; no GPU</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Build-and-size" tabindex="-1">Build and size <a class="header-anchor" href="#Build-and-size" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Build and size {#Build-and-size}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On macOS (arm64), the build output in <code>libfftl/build/lib</code> is 25 MB: the library (15 MB) and the Julia runtime next to it (10 MB). No Julia installation is needed to run a program that links it. The build needs Julia 1.12 or newer with <code>juliac</code>. See <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR6/howto/libfftl#C-library-(libfftl)">C library (libfftl)</a> for the build variables.</p>',18)])])}const u=t(l,[["render",r]]);export{p as __pageData,u as default};
